---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';

const faqs = [
  { q: "What is the eDistrict UP citizen login URL?", a: "The citizen login is at esathi.up.gov.in. You can also start from edistrict.up.gov.in and click the Citizen Login (eSathi) option. The old address edistrict.up.nic.in no longer works directly — it redirects to edistrict.up.gov.in." },
  { q: "Are the Login ID and mobile number the same thing?", a: "No. Your Login ID is the permanent 6–8 character username you chose during registration (for example, atul123). It is not your mobile number. If you forget it, use the Forgot Login ID option on the login page and it will be sent to your registered mobile by SMS." },
  { q: "How much does a certificate cost on eDistrict UP?", a: "Charges can vary by service and application channel. Treat the current amount displayed by the official portal as final. At a CSC, ask for the government/user charge and facilitation charge separately and insist on an itemised receipt." },
  { q: "How long does a certificate take to be issued?", a: "Most certificates take 7–15 working days. The Haisiyat certificate takes longer, around 15–21 days. Field verification by the Lekhpal is the slowest stage, and applications move slower during the July–September scholarship season." },
  { q: "Is a digitally signed certificate valid?", a: "A document issued and digitally signed by the competent authority can be verified as an electronic record. Acceptance, required issue date and format for a particular court, university, bank or recruitment follow that institution's current rules." },
  { q: "Are eDistrict UP and eSathi the same thing?", a: "They are two parts of one system. eDistrict is the back-end platform that connects district offices and departments, while eSathi is the citizen-facing portal where you register, log in, apply and download. When people say eDistrict UP login, they mean the eSathi citizen login." },
  { q: "Do I need Aadhaar to register on eSathi?", a: "Follow the identity and OTP method currently shown on the official registration screen. Keep an active mobile ready; if Aadhaar e-KYC is requested, complete it only through the official flow." },
  { q: "How do I check my application status without logging in?", a: "Open edistrict.up.gov.in, click Application Status (आवेदन की स्थिति), enter your Service Request Number (SRN) and the captcha, and search. The SRN was sent to you by SMS when you submitted the application and is also visible on your eSathi dashboard." },
  { q: "What is an SRN and how is it different from a certificate number?", a: "The SRN (Service Request Number) is the tracking number you receive at submission — use it on the status page. The Certificate Number is printed only on the final approved certificate and is what universities, employers and other third parties use for verification. The two numbers are different and not interchangeable." },
  { q: "I forgot my Login ID or password. What should I do?", a: "Use the links on the eSathi login page. For a forgotten password: Forgot Password, enter your mobile number, verify the OTP and set a new password. For a forgotten Login ID: the ID is sent to your registered mobile by SMS. If your mobile number itself has changed, contact the helpline on 0522-2304706 or email ceghelpdesk@gmail.com." },
  { q: "The OTP is not arriving. What can I check?", a: "Confirm you are checking the mobile number linked to Aadhaar, wait out the 2–3 minute cooldown before requesting another OTP, and look in the SMS spam folder. During the peak July–September season, early morning (6–8 AM) attempts usually succeed faster. Temporarily switching off DND can also help." },
  { q: "How do I download my certificate after approval?", a: "Use the official citizen account's issued/completed applications area and prefer the portal download control. Use DigiLocker only when the document appears under Issued Documents from the authoritative issuer; do not assume every service syncs automatically." },
  { q: "My application was rejected. What happens now?", a: "Log in to eSathi and read the rejection reason shown against your application. Common causes are blurry document scans, a name mismatch with Aadhaar, a missing self-declaration form, a wrong rural/urban selection, or the Lekhpal being unable to verify you at your address. Correct the issue and apply again — there is no penalty for reapplying." },
  { q: "How can a university or employer verify my certificate?", a: "Anyone can verify a certificate without logging in. On edistrict.up.gov.in, open Certificate Verification, enter the Certificate Number printed on the document (not the SRN), and match the details shown with the certificate. Scanning the QR code on the certificate opens the same verification page in seconds." },
  { q: "Why does my downloaded PDF show 'signature invalid'?", a: "This is a reader problem, not a certificate problem. Most PDF readers do not include Indian Certifying Authority root certificates in their trust stores, so they warn about the digital signature. Open the file in Adobe Acrobat Reader, click the signature and choose Validate Signature — a genuine certificate will show as valid." },
  { q: "What documents do I need for income, caste and domicile certificates?", a: "Identity, address, photograph, declaration and service-specific evidence are commonly requested. Read the current checklist and follow the size/format displayed for each upload field rather than assuming one universal 100 KB rule." },
  { q: "My application has been stuck for more than 15 days. What can I do?", a: "Start with a complaint on the portal itself, then call the helpline 0522-2304706 with your SRN ready. If it still does not move, raise it on CM Helpline 1076 or IGRS. Under the UP Janhit Guarantee Act, 2011, you can file a first appeal to the ADM within 30 days of the prescribed time limit expiring, and a second appeal to the DM within 60 days." },
  { q: "Can I get my certificate made at a CSC instead of applying myself?", a: "Yes. At a CSC or Lokvani centre, the operator applies on your behalf using their own VLE credentials (eseva.csccloud.in). You pay the government fee plus a facilitation charge, which is higher than the ₹10–15 self-service cost. Never share your personal eSathi Login ID or password with any operator — they do not need it." },
  { q: "How long is each certificate valid?", a: "Birth and death registrations are permanent civil records, but an institution can require a recently issued extract. For income, OBC-NCL, EWS, domicile and other certificates, follow the current issuing-authority rule and the notice of the institution receiving it." },
  { q: "Can I apply for a UP certificate from outside Uttar Pradesh?", a: "You can open the portal and apply from anywhere, but eligibility depends on the certificate. Domicile and caste certificates, for example, are issued on the basis of your Uttar Pradesh residence and records, and verification happens at your UP address — so the applicant must genuinely belong to Uttar Pradesh for those services." },
];
